69807.sol:
For an adiabatic process $\begin{aligned}& \mathrm{T}_{1} \mathrm{ V}_{1}^{\gamma-1}=\mathrm{T}_{2} \mathrm{ V}_{2}^{\gamma-1} \\
\therefore \quad & \frac{\mathrm{T}_2}{\mathrm{ T}_1}=\left(\frac{\mathrm{V}_1}{\mathrm{ V}_2}\right)^{\gamma-1}
\end{aligned}$ For a monoatomic gas, $r=\frac{5}{3} \begin{aligned}& \Rightarrow \gamma-1=\frac{5}{3}-1=\frac{2}{3}\\
& \mathrm{ V}_{1}= \mathrm{AL}_{1} \text{ and } \mathrm{V}_{2}= \mathrm{AL}_{2} \\
\therefore \quad & \frac{\mathrm{T}_2}{\mathrm{ T}_1}=\left[\frac{\mathrm{AL}_1}{\mathrm{AL}_2}\right]^{2 / 3}=\left[\frac{\mathrm{L}_1}{\mathrm{ L}_2}\right]^{2 / 3}\\
& \gamma-1=\frac{2}{3}
\end{aligned}$ For an adiabatic process, $\begin{aligned}& \frac{\mathrm{T}_2}{\mathrm{ T}_1}=\left(\frac{\mathrm{V}_1}{\mathrm{ V}_2}\right)^{\gamma-1}=\left(\frac{\mathrm{V}_1}{\mathrm{ V}_2}\right)^{\frac{2}{3}}\\
\mathrm{ V}& \propto \mathrm{L}\\
\therefore \quad \frac{\mathrm{T}_2}{\mathrm{ T}_1}& =\left(\frac{\mathrm{L}_1}{\mathrm{ L}_2}\right)^{\frac{2}{3}}
\end{aligned}$

